# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "A free IBM course on quantum information and computation"
msgstr "Um curso gratuito da IBM sobre informação e computação quântica"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bloch sphere"
msgstr "Esfera de Bloch"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"There's a useful geometric way to represent qubit states known as the *Bloch sphere*.\n"
"It's very convenient, but unfortunately it only works for qubits — the analogous representation no longer corresponds to a spherical object once we have three or more classical states of our system.\n"
msgstr ""
"Existe uma forma geométrica útil de representar estados de qubits, conhecida como a *esfera de Bloch*.\n"
"É muito conveniente, mas infelizmente só funciona para qubits — a representação análoga deixa de corresponder a um objeto esférico quando temos três ou mais estados clássicos no nosso sistema.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Qubit states as points on a sphere {#qubit-states-as-points-on-a-sphere}"
msgstr "Estados de qubits como pontos em uma esfera {#qubit-states-as-points-on-a-sphere}"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's start by thinking about a quantum state vector of a qubit: $\\alpha \\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta \\vert 1\\rangle$.\n"
"We can restrict our attention to vectors for which $\\alpha$ is a nonnegative real number because every qubit state vector is equivalent up to a global phase to one for which $\\alpha \\geq 0$.\n"
"This allows us to write\n"
msgstr ""
"Vamos começar pensando em um vetor de estado quântico de um qubit: $\\alpha \\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta \\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"
"Podemos restringir nossa atenção a vetores para os quais $\\alpha$ é um número real não negativo, pois todo vetor de estado quântico de um qubit é equivalente, a menos de uma fase global, a um para o qual $\\alpha \\geq 0.$\n"
"Isso nos permite escrever\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle = \\cos\\bigl(\\theta/2\\bigr) \\vert 0\\rangle + e^{i\\phi} \\sin\\bigl(\\theta/2\\bigr) \\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle = \\cos\\bigl(\\theta/2\\bigr) \\vert 0\\rangle + e^{i\\phi} \\sin\\bigl(\\theta/2\\bigr) \\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for two real numbers $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi]$ and $\\phi\\in[0,2\\pi)$.\n"
"Here, we're allowing $\\theta$ to range from $0$ to $\\pi$ and dividing by $2$ in the argument of sine and cosine because this is a conventional way to parameterize vectors of this sort, and it will make things simpler a bit later on.\n"
msgstr ""
"para dois números reais $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi]$ e $\\phi\\in[0,2\\pi)$.\n"
"Aqui, permitimos que $\\theta$ varie de $0$ a $\\pi$ e dividimos por $2$ no argumento do seno e do cosseno porque essa é uma forma convencional de parametrizar vetores desse tipo, e tornará as coisas mais simples um pouco mais adiante.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, it isn't quite the case that the numbers $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ are uniquely determined by a given quantum state vector $\\alpha \\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta \\vert 1\\rangle$, but it is nearly so.\n"
"In particular, if $\\beta = 0$, then $\\theta = 0$ and it doesn't make any difference what value $\\phi$ takes, so it can be chosen arbitrarily.\n"
"Similarly, if $\\alpha = 0$, then $\\theta = \\pi$, and once again $\\phi$ is irrelevant (as our state is equivalent to $e^{i\\phi}\\vert 1\\rangle$ for any $\\phi$ up to a global phase).\n"
"If, however, neither $\\alpha$ nor $\\beta$ is zero, then there's a unique choice for the pair $(\\theta,\\phi)$ for which $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ is equivalent to $\\alpha\\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta\\vert 1\\rangle$ up to a global phase.\n"
msgstr ""
"Agora, não é bem o caso de que os números $\\theta$ e $\\phi$ sejam unicamente determinados por um dado vetor de estado quântico $\\alpha \\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta \\vert 1\\rangle$, mas é quase isso.\n"
"Em particular, se $\\beta = 0$, então $\\theta = 0$ e não faz diferença qual valor $\\phi$ assume, então ele pode ser escolhido arbitrariamente.\n"
"Da mesma forma, se $\\alpha = 0$, então $\\theta = \\pi$, e mais uma vez $\\phi$ é irrelevante (pois nosso estado é equivalente a $e^{i\\phi}\\vert 1\\rangle$ para qualquer $\\phi$ a menos de uma fase global).\n"
"Se, no entanto, nem $\\alpha$ nem $\\beta$ for zero, então há uma escolha única para o par $(\\theta,\\phi)$ para a qual $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ é equivalente a $\\alpha\\vert 0\\rangle + \\beta\\vert 1\\rangle$ a menos de uma fase global.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Next, let's consider the density matrix representation of this state.\n"
msgstr "A seguir, vamos considerar a representação por matriz densidade desse estado.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\theta/2) & e^{-i\\phi}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\sin(\\theta/2)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"e^{i\\phi}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\sin(\\theta/2) & \\sin^2(\\theta/2)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\theta/2) & e^{-i\\phi}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\sin(\\theta/2)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"e^{i\\phi}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\sin(\\theta/2) & \\sin^2(\\theta/2)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can use some trigonometric identities,\n"
msgstr "Podemos usar algumas identidades trigonométricas,\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\theta/2) = \\frac{1 + \\cos(\\theta)}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\sin^2(\\theta/2) = \\frac{1 - \\cos(\\theta)}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\cos(\\theta/2) \\sin(\\theta/2) = \\frac{\\sin(\\theta)}{2},\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\theta/2) = \\frac{1 + \\cos(\\theta)}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\sin^2(\\theta/2) = \\frac{1 - \\cos(\\theta)}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\cos(\\theta/2) \\sin(\\theta/2) = \\frac{\\sin(\\theta)}{2},\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "as well as the formula $e^{i\\phi} = \\cos(\\phi) + i\\sin(\\phi)$, to simplify the density matrix as follows.\n"
msgstr "bem como a fórmula $e^{i\\phi} = \\cos(\\phi) + i\\sin(\\phi)$, para simplificar a matriz densidade da seguinte forma.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 + \\cos(\\theta) & (\\cos(\\phi) - i \\sin(\\phi)) \\sin(\\theta)\\\\[1mm]\n"
"(\\cos(\\phi) + i \\sin(\\phi)) \\sin(\\theta) & 1 - \\cos(\\theta)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 + \\cos(\\theta) & (\\cos(\\phi) - i \\sin(\\phi)) \\sin(\\theta)\\\\[1mm]\n"
"(\\cos(\\phi) + i \\sin(\\phi)) \\sin(\\theta) & 1 - \\cos(\\theta)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This makes it easy to express this density matrix as a linear combination of the Pauli matrices:\n"
msgstr "Isso torna fácil expressar essa matriz densidade como uma combinação linear das matrizes de Pauli:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"  \\mathbb{I} =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    0 & 1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_x =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    0 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    1 & 0\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_y =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    0 & -i\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    i & 0\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_z =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    0 & -1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"  \\mathbb{I} =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    0 & 1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_x =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    0 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    1 & 0\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_y =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    0 & -i\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    i & 0\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix},\n"
"  \\quad\n"
"  \\sigma_z =\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"    1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"    0 & -1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Specifically, we conclude that\n"
msgstr "Especificamente, concluímos que\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sin(\\theta) \\cos(\\phi)\\sigma_x + \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\phi) \\sigma_y + \\cos(\\theta) \\sigma_z}{2}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sin(\\theta) \\cos(\\phi)\\sigma_x + \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\phi) \\sigma_y + \\cos(\\theta) \\sigma_z}{2}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The coefficients of $\\sigma_x$, $\\sigma_y$, and $\\sigma_z$ in the numerator of this expression are all real numbers, so we can collect them together to form a vector in an ordinary, three-dimensional Euclidean space.\n"
msgstr "Os coeficientes de $\\sigma_x,$ $\\sigma_y,$ e $\\sigma_z$ no numerador dessa expressão são todos números reais, então podemos reuni-los para formar um vetor em um espaço euclidiano tridimensional ordinário.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\bigl(\\sin(\\theta) \\cos(\\phi), \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\phi), \\cos(\\theta)\\bigr)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\bigl(\\sin(\\theta) \\cos(\\phi), \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\phi), \\cos(\\theta)\\bigr)\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In fact, this is a unit vector.\n"
"Using *spherical coordinates* it can be written as $(1,\\theta,\\phi)$.\n"
"The first coordinate, $1$, represents the *radius* or *radial distance* (which is always $1$ in this case), $\\theta$ represents the *polar angle*, and $\\phi$ represents the *azimuthal angle*.\n"
msgstr ""
"Na verdade, esse é um vetor unitário.\n"
"Usando *coordenadas esféricas*, ele pode ser escrito como $(1,\\theta,\\phi).$\n"
"A primeira coordenada, $1,$ representa o *raio* ou *distância radial* (que é sempre $1$ neste caso), $\\theta$ representa o *ângulo polar*, e $\\phi$ representa o *ângulo azimutal*.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In words, thinking about a sphere as the planet Earth, the polar angle $\\theta$ is how far we rotate south from the north pole to reach the point being described, from $0$ to $\\pi = 180^{\\circ}$, while the azimuthal angle $\\phi$ is how far we rotate east from the prime meridian, from $0$ to $2\\pi = 360^{\\circ}$.\n"
"This assumes that we define the prime meridian to be the curve on the surface of the sphere from one pole to the other that passes through the positive $x$-axis.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para entender melhor, pensando em uma esfera como o planeta Terra, o ângulo polar $\\theta$ é o quanto rotacionamos para o sul a partir do polo norte para alcançar o ponto descrito, de $0$ a $\\pi = 180^{\\circ}$, enquanto o ângulo azimutal $\\phi$ é o quanto rotacionamos para o leste a partir do meridiano principal, de $0$ a $2\\pi = 360^{\\circ}$.\n"
"Isso pressupõe que definimos o meridiano principal como a curva na superfície da esfera de um polo ao outro que passa pelo eixo $x$ positivo.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Illustration of a point on the unit 2-sphere in terms of its spherical coordinates.](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/spherical-coordinates.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Ilustração de um ponto na 2-esfera unitária em termos de suas coordenadas esféricas.](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/spherical-coordinates.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every point on the sphere can be described in this way — which is to say that the points we obtain when we range over all possible pure states of a qubit correspond precisely to a sphere in $3$ real dimensions.\n"
"(This sphere is typically called the *unit $2$-sphere* because the surface of this sphere is two-dimensional.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Todo ponto na esfera pode ser descrito dessa forma — o que significa que os pontos que obtemos ao variar sobre todos os estados puros possíveis de um qubit correspondem precisamente a uma esfera em $3$ dimensões reais.\n"
"(Essa esfera é tipicamente chamada de *$2$-esfera unitária* porque a superfície dessa esfera é bidimensional.)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "When we associate points on the unit $2$-sphere with pure states of qubits, we obtain the *Bloch sphere* representation these states.\n"
msgstr "Quando associamos pontos na $2$-esfera unitária a estados puros de qubits, obtemos a representação pela *esfera de Bloch* desses estados.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Six important examples {#six-important-examples}"
msgstr "Seis exemplos importantes {#six-important-examples}"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*The standard basis* $\\{\\vert 0\\rangle,\\vert 1\\rangle\\}$.\n"
"Let's start with the state $\\vert 0\\rangle$.\n"
"As a density matrix it can be written like this.\n"
msgstr ""
"*A base padrão* $\\{\\vert 0\\rangle,\\vert 1\\rangle\\}$.\n"
"Vamos começar com o estado $\\vert 0\\rangle$.\n"
"Como matriz densidade, ele pode ser escrito assim.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 0 \\rangle \\langle 0 \\vert = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 0 \\rangle \\langle 0 \\vert = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   By collecting the coefficients of the Pauli matrices in the numerator, we see that the corresponding point on the unit $2$-sphere using Cartesian coordinates is $(0,0,1)$.\n"
"   In spherical coordinates this point is $(1,0,\\phi)$, where $\\phi$ can be any angle.\n"
"   This is consistent with the expression\n"
msgstr ""
"   Ao reunir os coeficientes das matrizes de Pauli no numerador, vemos que o ponto correspondente na $2$-esfera unitária em coordenadas cartesianas é $(0,0,1)$.\n"
"   Em coordenadas esféricas, esse ponto é $(1,0,\\phi)$, onde $\\phi$ pode ser qualquer ângulo.\n"
"   Isso é consistente com a expressão\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 0\\rangle = \\cos(0) \\vert 0\\rangle + e^{i \\phi} \\sin(0) \\vert 1\\rangle,\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 0\\rangle = \\cos(0) \\vert 0\\rangle + e^{i \\phi} \\sin(0) \\vert 1\\rangle,\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   which also works for any $\\phi$.\n"
"   Intuitively speaking, the polar angle $\\theta$ is zero, so we're at the north pole of the Bloch sphere, where the azimuthal angle is irrelevant.\n"
msgstr ""
"   que também vale para qualquer $\\phi$.\n"
"   Intuitivamente, o ângulo polar $\\theta$ é zero, então estamos no polo norte da esfera de Bloch, onde o ângulo azimutal é irrelevante.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   Along similar lines, the density matrix for the state $\\vert 1\\rangle$ can be written like so.\n"
msgstr "   De forma análoga, a matriz densidade para o estado $\\vert 1\\rangle$ pode ser escrita assim.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 1 \\rangle \\langle 1 \\vert = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\vert 1 \\rangle \\langle 1 \\vert = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   This time the Cartesian coordinates are $(0,0,-1)$. In spherical coordinates this point is $(1,\\pi,\\phi)$ where $\\phi$ can be any angle. In this case the polar angle is all the way to $\\pi$, so we're at the south pole where the azimuthal angle is again irrelevant.\n"
msgstr "   Desta vez, as coordenadas cartesianas são $(0,0,-1)$. Em coordenadas esféricas, esse ponto é $(1,\\pi,\\phi)$ onde $\\phi$ pode ser qualquer ângulo. Neste caso, o ângulo polar vai até $\\pi$, então estamos no polo sul onde o ângulo azimutal é novamente irrelevante.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*The basis $\\{\\vert + \\rangle, \\vert - \\rangle\\}$.*\n"
"We have these expressions for the density matrices corresponding to these states.\n"
msgstr ""
"*A base $\\{\\vert + \\rangle, \\vert - \\rangle\\}$.*\n"
"Temos essas expressões para as matrizes densidade correspondentes a esses estados.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+} \\rangle\\langle {+} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_x}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-} \\rangle\\langle {-} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_x}{2}\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+} \\rangle\\langle {+} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_x}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-} \\rangle\\langle {-} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_x}{2}\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   The corresponding points on the unit $2$-sphere have Cartesian coordinates $(1,0,0)$ and $(-1,0,0)$,\n"
"   and spherical coordinates $(1,\\pi/2,0)$ and $(1,\\pi/2,\\pi)$, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""
"   Os pontos correspondentes na $2$-esfera unitária têm coordenadas cartesianas $(1,0,0)$ e $(-1,0,0)$,\n"
"   e coordenadas esféricas $(1,\\pi/2,0)$ e $(1,\\pi/2,\\pi)$, respectivamente.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   In words, $\\vert +\\rangle$ corresponds to the point where the positive $x$-axis intersects the unit $2$-sphere and $\\vert -\\rangle$ corresponds to the point where the negative $x$-axis intersects it. More intuitively, $\\vert +\\rangle$ is on the equator of the Bloch sphere where it meets the prime meridian, and $\\vert - \\rangle$ is on the equator on the opposite side of the sphere.\n"
msgstr "   Em palavras, $\\vert +\\rangle$ corresponde ao ponto onde o eixo $x$ positivo intersecta a $2$-esfera unitária e $\\vert -\\rangle$ corresponde ao ponto onde o eixo $x$ negativo a intersecta. Mais intuitivamente, $\\vert +\\rangle$ está no equador da esfera de Bloch onde ela encontra o meridiano principal, e $\\vert - \\rangle$ está no equador do lado oposto da esfera.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*The basis* $\\{\\vert {+i} \\rangle, \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\}$.\n"
"As we saw earlier in the lesson, these two states are defined like this:\n"
msgstr ""
"*A base* $\\{\\vert {+i} \\rangle, \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\}$.\n"
"Como vimos anteriormente na lição, esses dois estados são definidos assim:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle + \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle - \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle.\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle + \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle - \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle.\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   This time we have these expressions.\n"
msgstr "   Desta vez temos essas expressões.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_y}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_y}{2}\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"
msgstr ""
"   $$\n"
"   \\begin{aligned}\n"
"   \\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sigma_y}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"   \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert & = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} - \\sigma_y}{2}\n"
"   \\end{aligned}\n"
"   $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   The corresponding points on the unit $2$-sphere have Cartesian coordinates $(0,1,0)$ and $(0,-1,0)$,\n"
"   and spherical coordinates $(1,\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$ and $(1,\\pi/2,3\\pi/2)$, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""
"   Os pontos correspondentes na $2$-esfera unitária têm coordenadas cartesianas $(0,1,0)$ e $(0,-1,0)$,\n"
"   e coordenadas esféricas $(1,\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$ e $(1,\\pi/2,3\\pi/2)$, respectivamente.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "   In words, $\\vert {+i} \\rangle$ corresponds to the point where the positive $y$-axis intersects the unit $2$-sphere and $\\vert {-i} \\rangle$ to the point where the negative $y$-axis intersects it.\n"
msgstr "   Em palavras, $\\vert {+i} \\rangle$ corresponde ao ponto onde o eixo $y$ positivo intersecta a $2$-esfera unitária e $\\vert {-i} \\rangle$ ao ponto onde o eixo $y$ negativo a intersecta.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Illustration of six examples of pure states on the Bloch sphere](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/six-Bloch-sphere-points.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Ilustração de seis exemplos de estados puros na esfera de Bloch](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/six-Bloch-sphere-points.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here's another class of quantum state vectors that has appeared from time to time throughout this series, including previously in this lesson.\n"
msgstr "Aqui está outra classe de vetores de estado quântico que apareceu de tempos em tempos ao longo desta série, incluindo anteriormente nesta lição.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\psi_{\\alpha} \\rangle = \\cos(\\alpha) \\vert 0\\rangle + \\sin(\\alpha) \\vert 1\\rangle \\qquad \\text{(for $\\alpha \\in [0,\\pi)$)}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\psi_{\\alpha} \\rangle = \\cos(\\alpha) \\vert 0\\rangle + \\sin(\\alpha) \\vert 1\\rangle \\qquad \\text{(para $\\alpha \\in [0,\\pi)$)}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The density matrix representation of each of these states is as follows.\n"
msgstr "A representação por matriz densidade de cada um desses estados é a seguinte.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\psi_{\\alpha} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\alpha} \\vert =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\alpha) & \\cos(\\alpha)\\sin(\\alpha)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\cos(\\alpha)\\sin(\\alpha) & \\sin^2(\\alpha)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sin(2\\alpha) \\sigma_x + \\cos(2\\alpha) \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\psi_{\\alpha} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\alpha} \\vert =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\cos^2(\\alpha) & \\cos(\\alpha)\\sin(\\alpha)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\cos(\\alpha)\\sin(\\alpha) & \\sin^2(\\alpha)\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + \\sin(2\\alpha) \\sigma_x + \\cos(2\\alpha) \\sigma_z}{2}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following figure illustrates the corresponding points on the Bloch sphere for a few choices for $\\alpha$.\n"
msgstr "A figura a seguir ilustra os pontos correspondentes na esfera de Bloch para algumas escolhas de $\\alpha$.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Illustration of real-valued qubit state vectors on the Bloch sphere](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/real-Bloch-sphere-points.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Ilustração de vetores de estado quântico de valor real na esfera de Bloch](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/real-Bloch-sphere-points.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Convex combinations of points {#convex-combinations-of-points}"
msgstr "Combinações convexas de pontos {#convex-combinations-of-points}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Similar to what we have already discussed for density matrices, we can take convex combinations of points on the Bloch sphere to obtain representations of qubit density matrices.\n"
"In general, this results in points *inside* of the Bloch sphere, which represent density matrices of states that are not pure.\n"
"Sometimes we refer to the *Bloch ball* when we wish to be explicit about the inclusion of points inside of the Bloch sphere as representations of qubit density matrices.\n"
msgstr ""
"De forma semelhante ao que já discutimos para matrizes densidade, podemos tomar combinações convexas de pontos na esfera de Bloch para obter representações de matrizes densidade de qubits.\n"
"Em geral, isso resulta em pontos *dentro* da esfera de Bloch, que representam matrizes densidade de estados que não são puros.\n"
"Às vezes nos referimos à *bola de Bloch* quando queremos ser explícitos sobre a inclusão de pontos no interior da esfera de Bloch como representações de matrizes densidade de qubits.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For example, we've seen that the density matrix $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbb{I}$, which represents the completely mixed state of a qubit, can be written in these two alternative ways:\n"
msgstr "Por exemplo, vimos que a matriz densidade $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbb{I}$, que representa o estado completamente misto de um qubit, pode ser escrita dessas duas formas alternativas:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert\n"
"\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle +\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert -\\rangle\\langle -\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert\n"
"\\quad\\text{e}\\quad\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle +\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert -\\rangle\\langle -\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We also have\n"
msgstr "Também temos\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} =\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert {+i}\\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2} \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i}\\vert,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} =\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert {+i}\\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2} \\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i}\\vert,\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"and more generally we can use any two orthogonal qubit state vectors (which will always correspond to two antipodal points on the Bloch sphere).\n"
"If we average the corresponding points on the Bloch sphere in a similar way, we obtain the same point, which in this case is at the center of the sphere.\n"
"This is consistent with the observation that\n"
msgstr ""
"e, de forma mais geral, podemos usar quaisquer dois vetores de estado quântico de qubits ortogonais (que sempre corresponderão a dois pontos antipodais na esfera de Bloch).\n"
"Se calcularmos a média dos pontos correspondentes na esfera de Bloch de maneira similar, obtemos o mesmo ponto, que neste caso está no centro da esfera.\n"
"Isso é consistente com a observação de que\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_x + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_y + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_z}{2},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I} = \\frac{\\mathbb{I} + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_x + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_y + 0 \\cdot \\sigma_z}{2},\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "giving us the Cartesian coordinates $(0,0,0)$.\n"
msgstr "nos dando as coordenadas cartesianas $(0,0,0)$.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A different example concerning convex combinations of Bloch sphere points is the one discussed in the previous subsection.\n"
msgstr "Um exemplo diferente sobre combinações convexas de pontos na esfera de Bloch é o discutido na subseção anterior.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0 \\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle + \\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\cos^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert +\n"
"\\sin^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{5\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0 \\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle + \\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\cos^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert +\n"
"\\sin^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{5\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following figure illustrates these two different ways of obtaining this density matrix as a convex combination of pure states.\n"
msgstr "A figura a seguir ilustra essas duas formas diferentes de obter essa matriz densidade como uma combinação convexa de estados puros.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Illustration of the average of the zero state and the plus state on the Bloch sphere](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/Bloch-sphere-zero-plus-average.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Ilustração da média do estado zero e do estado mais na esfera de Bloch](/learning/images/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/density-matrices/Bloch-sphere-zero-plus-average.avif)\n"
